00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.199
know professional or at least
semi-professional I think my first was

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:32.640
in 99 or 2000 I took about a four year
break once I moved to Los Angeles didn't

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.800
know anybody didn't have any money so I
needed to put some pieces together and

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:45.539
once I did that that probably was 2005
and I think my last short film to date

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:51.539
at least would have been a fun little
crime action film called sweet madness

00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:59.399
which I believe was 2015 so good solid
ten years of just non-student and we're

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:02.879
not talking student films or kid films
like me going out there pulling in

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:06.330
investors and producers pulling the true
together and just getting it done

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:11.549
there's probably good solid ten eleven
years so that four years that you came

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:16.439
here and you weren't making films what
was that light because you you'd planned

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:20.850
making films since you were a kid
go to film school in a really cool town

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:26.340
and and then you come here and
essentially you probably feel like oh

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:32.909
wow now I'm gonna be living what I set
out to do well reality hits as much as I

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:37.320
love the film school again it didn't
prepare you to go into this industry not

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.750
Los Angeles I came out in a sense
cold turkey I'd been taught how to load

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:48.869
some mags and I wrote like a short film
script of my senior film class but I

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:51.600
didn't know how to pull the crew
together I didn't know how to produce I

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.430
didn't know about permits I didn't know
anything about any of it I knew I had to

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.689
learn it though I also didn't have any
money I had to get a job pretty soon you

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:07.500
find yourself working a job or two and
then I tried I was doing that I got some

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.860
internships on the side to try to meet
people figure out how the business work

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.090
I eventually got a job at a
literary agency representing writers and

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.450
directors and so on and so forth
to learn that side of the business I

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.110
started to put together my long term
plan at that point I wanted to know who

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:27.000
was who in the town I wanted to know how
contracts worked what was fair what

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.190
wasn't fair I wanted to learn how these
people got representation what it took

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.580
and it just took time to get these
things in place and while I was doing

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.450
these jobs I had access to no gear I
didn't have money to pay for gear I

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.030
didn't know people to ask they'd come in
volunteer

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.380
I had no press no brand to sell myself
to get people to want to work with me

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:51.450
for nothing so it took a number of years
to get to a place I was like okay I am

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.660
as close as I'm going to be I mean I
couldn't wait another 10 years but I'm

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:00.290
as close as I'm going to be to having
the ability to do my first post-college

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:05.280
film and that was around 2005 I just
eventually just pulled the trigger I so

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.959
I'm doing it I'll figure out the rest at
some point you just got to go with it

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:12.799
I'm and so I did I made that film for
about seven or eight thousand dollars

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:18.450
that was the last film I shot on actual
film after that everything went digital

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:23.760
and it saved me a tremendous amount of
money but yeah I that's something that

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.670
that film students and everything canon
should be aware of in terms of of me I

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.880
just didn't know any better I went to a
film school that taught me some amazing

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.500
things about the history of film and I'm
so glad I learned it but I came out here

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.590
like a baby I didn't know anybody didn't
know anything and had to learn

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.780
everything on my own from the ground up
which has proved beneficial to me in the

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:51.810
long run but at first it was challenging
you just had to find your way and just

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.450
go with it
you know with film and you could read a

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.889
million books but there's no simple a
twosie about how to make it or how to be

00:03:58.889 --> 00:04:04.560
a producer or director a writer so I
kind of did it the only way I knew how I

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.139
learned how on set and whatever mistakes
I made I learned from them and didn't do

00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:14.549
them again
but it it's and everyone's got their own

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:19.440
road that was mine so yeah I had that
little four year pause and once that

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.960
pause ended I've been pumping out films
ever since were you working at the

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.910
literary agency the entire for you
know what ended up I will know I worked

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.570
at the literary agency for about a year
and a half I interned at Paramount I

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:39.630
interned for some other producers in
town I worked at why don't you remember

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.980
the name of I worked at the mall I
worked at restaurants I just worked you

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:50.130
got to make your money oh but that
taught me early on one of the most

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.730
important lessons which for me I always
knew what I wanted to do there was no

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.540
doubt about it and I was prepared for it
to be an uphill battle for the rest of

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:03.300
my life and it has been I mean being a
filmmaker is hard in all the best ways

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.410
to me I think everything you really love
and care about it's hard you know it

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.680
takes a lot of effort and filmmaking
certainly requires a lot of effort but

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.060
as I've been out here in Los Angeles
steadily working away and putting my

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.230
pieces in place and kind of figuring out
okay where is this gonna lead me in five

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.350
years versus ten years like where do I
want to go and how am I gonna get there

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:28.440
it took a while for me to figure out
exactly what I needed to do but

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.360
throughout that journey I saw a lot of
people come out wanting to do the same

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.180
they wouldn't make it very long and one
of the most common traits I saw and the

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.680
people who didn't make it very long were
those who I don't think fully

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.790
appreciated how hard filmmaking is I
think they loved film I don't think they

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:51.660
loved filmmaking
and it's an important difference I think

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:56.760
that if you come out to this town with
stars in your eyes and some of these

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.130
things I've written about before which
you might have read some of my articles

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:03.990
but I think if you come out to LA to be
a filmmaker and kind of the preeminent

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:10.560
